EXT. LIFE RAFT - OPEN OCEAN - DAY

A yellow raft on a flat blue sea. The sun is a hammer. A tattered canopy offers a stripe of shade. Three castaways sit in a triangle, rationing silence.

CAPTAIN LUCIAN BRACKENRIDGE, fifties, sits at the bow with a sextant he no longer needs, his white uniform gone gray. At the stern, MARIANNE DELACROIX-SOTO, thirties, ship's doctor, tends a water pouch with the care of a priest. Between them, hunched over his knees, is ANDERS TELLEFSEN, twenties, deckhand, lips cracked, eyes fixed on a point on the horizon that isn't there.

Lucian holds out a hand. Marianne passes the pouch. He sips, and passes it to Anders. Anders holds it and does not drink.

LUCIAN
Drink, son.

ANDERS
Later.

LUCIAN
Now.

ANDERS
Later, Captain.

Marianne watches. She says nothing. A wave slaps the hull, and spray flies over their faces and dries almost at once.

MARIANNE ^
Anders.

LUCIAN ^
Anders.

Anders looks up at them both, then down at the pouch. He lifts it, slowly, as if lifting a weight, and drinks. Only a sip. He hands it back.

Marianne takes it. She tilts it to the light. It is almost empty.

She looks at Lucian. He looks at the sea.

MARIANNE
(quietly)
Two days.

LUCIAN
Two days.

ANDERS
You're lying.

Silence. A gull circles overhead, wheeling in the cloudless blue.

ANDERS (CONT'D)
There's one day. Maybe less.

LUCIAN
Anders --

ANDERS
You both knew.

Marianne starts to answer. Lucian puts a hand on her wrist. She stops. Anders is on his knees now, the raft rocking dangerously under him, and he lunges for the pouch. Marianne pulls it away. Lucian seizes his arm. The three of them crash together in a tangle, and the pouch splits, and the last of the water spills into the bottom of the raft and is gone.

Nobody moves.

Anders slumps back. Marianne stares at the dark stain on the rubber. Lucian lets go of Anders's arm and, very gently, closes his own eyes.

LUCIAN (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Well.

The gull wheels overhead, close now. Marianne tilts her head back and looks up at it for a long time.
